WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.792 --> 00:00:04.262
Hi, I'm Michelle Klein, a member of the
GSB Alumni Association Board.

00:00:04.262 --> 00:00:07.671
And I'm pleased to welcome you to this
production.

00:00:07.671 --> 00:00:08.716
&gt;&gt; [FOREIGN]
&gt;&gt; Change lives.

00:00:08.716 --> 00:00:10.138
&gt;&gt; Change organization.

00:00:10.138 --> 00:00:11.537
&gt;&gt; [FOREIGN]
&gt;&gt; Change organization.

00:00:11.537 --> 00:00:12.460
[FOREIGN]
&gt;&gt; Change the world.

00:00:12.460 --> 00:00:13.711
[MUSIC]

00:00:13.711 --> 00:00:20.051
&gt;&gt; Well, thank you for the awesome
introduction, I appreciate that.

00:00:20.051 --> 00:00:21.850
I was super nervous.

00:00:21.850 --> 00:00:24.533
And then you all started filing in and
then I was like, these

00:00:24.533 --> 00:00:27.229
are all my great friends that I made over
the last two years.

00:00:27.229 --> 00:00:29.995
And so I'm just so honored to be here.

00:00:29.995 --> 00:00:34.743
And I am so grateful that you are
entrusting me with this last lecture.

00:00:34.743 --> 00:00:39.284
It's just an incredible honor, it means
the world to me.

00:00:39.284 --> 00:00:41.572
So thank you for that.

00:00:41.572 --> 00:00:45.125
[COUGH] I want to say congratulations to
all of you, not

00:00:45.125 --> 00:00:48.273
just on the last couple of years, but
really on

00:00:48.273 --> 00:00:51.988
your whole lives, the 25 years or 30 years
leading

00:00:51.988 --> 00:00:55.742
to getting to Stanford and everything you
put into it.

00:00:55.742 --> 00:00:59.442
The incredible intensity, work, and
sacrifices you made, the

00:00:59.442 --> 00:01:02.335
decisions you made, and the grit that you
showed to

00:01:02.335 --> 00:01:04.622
get here, I hope you feel like you earned

00:01:04.622 --> 00:01:07.662
the seat that you're sitting in and you
belong here.

00:01:07.662 --> 00:01:11.482
And I just hope you'll over the next
couple of days appreciate

00:01:11.482 --> 00:01:13.612
really what you've done because it's

00:01:13.612 --> 00:01:16.706
really impressive, give yourselves a big
hand.

00:01:16.706 --> 00:01:24.248
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]
&gt;&gt; The last lecture I took very seriously,

00:01:24.248 --> 00:01:27.461
I said, this is my last lecture I'm ever
going to give, what am I going to say?

00:01:27.461 --> 00:01:29.081
I'm going to try to tie in things we

00:01:29.081 --> 00:01:31.361
talked about in class, things I talked
about

00:01:31.361 --> 00:01:33.581
in wellness, and I'm going to talk about
even

00:01:33.581 --> 00:01:36.053
stuff we did during lunches and stuff like
that.

00:01:36.053 --> 00:01:39.397
I'm going to do all that in 25 minutes.

00:01:39.397 --> 00:01:41.779
Let's see how it goes.

00:01:41.779 --> 00:01:45.981
So I turned 50 this year, and as many of
you who know, who have had me

00:01:45.981 --> 00:01:49.317
in class, I'm a pretty reflective person,
and I've

00:01:49.317 --> 00:01:52.384
thought about what have I learned over
that time.

00:01:52.384 --> 00:01:55.369
As Drake said, I've been on many journeys,

00:01:55.369 --> 00:01:58.289
had lots of failures and some stuff go
well.

00:01:58.289 --> 00:02:02.879
And what I realized at the end of all that
is that life is really the

00:02:02.879 --> 00:02:06.279
most challenging journey of all that we're
all

00:02:06.279 --> 00:02:09.179
going to have and that all of you will
have.

00:02:09.179 --> 00:02:12.722
And then what I've realized over 50 years
is it's really an internal journey.

00:02:12.722 --> 00:02:15.818
It's one that you're going to really be
wrestling with yourself over that time.

00:02:15.818 --> 00:02:20.782
And there's a quote that I love that
summarizes this journey

00:02:20.782 --> 00:02:25.858
better than anything I could come up with
from Michelangelo.

00:02:25.858 --> 00:02:30.050
And Michelangelo sculpted the David and he
painted the Sistine Chapel.

00:02:30.050 --> 00:02:33.563
And one of his early works was this angel.

00:02:33.563 --> 00:02:38.310
People had never seen this intricacy and
detail in this angel before.

00:02:38.310 --> 00:02:42.695
And they asked him, how did you sculpt
this angel out of this block of stone?

00:02:42.695 --> 00:02:46.894
And Michelangelo said, famously, I saw the
angel in

00:02:46.894 --> 00:02:50.266
the marble and I carved until I set it
free.

00:02:50.266 --> 00:02:55.234
The reason that quote is so important and
that it's my favorite

00:02:55.234 --> 00:03:00.027
quote is that we, all of us, we're the
angel in the marble.

00:03:00.027 --> 00:03:06.580
And that we at our core are these
beautiful, infinite, incredible beings.

00:03:06.580 --> 00:03:09.316
We have power beyond our wildest
imagination.

00:03:09.316 --> 00:03:13.264
And we're perfect just as we are.

00:03:13.264 --> 00:03:19.694
And that the real journey is to sculpt
away the stone that bring case in, and

00:03:19.694 --> 00:03:26.962
most of the most constraining stone that
we have, actually, it's self directed.

00:03:26.962 --> 00:03:29.890
It's the constraints we put on ourselves.

00:03:29.890 --> 00:03:33.148
And it's things like, here's what my
parents think I should do.

00:03:33.148 --> 00:03:34.791
Here's what my roommate's doing.

00:03:34.791 --> 00:03:38.862
I compare myself to this person and I
compare myself unfavorably.

00:03:38.862 --> 00:03:40.108
I can't do this.

00:03:40.108 --> 00:03:41.723
I don't know how to do this.

00:03:41.723 --> 00:03:43.503
I'm not sure I'm going to make it.

00:03:43.503 --> 00:03:46.303
I might not work out, I might fail.

00:03:46.303 --> 00:03:47.584
And I'm not ready.

00:03:47.584 --> 00:03:49.120
I'm not enough.

00:03:49.120 --> 00:03:51.550
And really, that's what's underneath a lot
of that, is I'm not enough.

00:03:51.550 --> 00:03:56.407
So that's a stone that we kind of place on
ourselves.

00:03:56.407 --> 00:03:59.447
There's a subtle part of this quote that I
want to

00:03:59.447 --> 00:04:02.411
make sure that I make like not subtle,
which is

00:04:02.411 --> 00:04:06.364
that Michelangelo's didn't sculpt the
angel or the David by

00:04:06.364 --> 00:04:09.263
going out and finding a head and gluing it
on.

00:04:09.263 --> 00:04:13.619
And then finding wings and then welding
those on.

00:04:13.619 --> 00:04:16.162
The angel was already perfect.

00:04:16.162 --> 00:04:19.546
And that really if I had a gift that I
could give to you, it would be

00:04:19.546 --> 00:04:21.931
for you to just give yourself permission
to be

00:04:21.931 --> 00:04:24.465
your true selves over the course of your
lives.

00:04:24.465 --> 00:04:27.088
That would be the gift that I would give
you.

00:04:27.088 --> 00:04:30.177
That's what I've learned over my 50 years,
is

00:04:30.177 --> 00:04:33.043
that when you give yourself that gift, you
tap

00:04:33.043 --> 00:04:36.129
into this energy source and this joy and
happiness

00:04:36.129 --> 00:04:39.534
and really power that you can't access any
other way.

00:04:39.534 --> 00:04:42.590
You can't access it by trying to be
someone else.

00:04:42.590 --> 00:04:44.948
So what I'm going to do today is take you

00:04:44.948 --> 00:04:47.763
on a little bit of a journey to,
hopefully,

00:04:47.763 --> 00:04:51.034
help you unlock your potential, kind of
carve away

00:04:51.034 --> 00:04:53.944
that marble over the next 25 minutes or
so.

00:04:53.944 --> 00:04:58.721
And I'm going to use my own story to tell
you a little bit about this.

00:04:58.721 --> 00:05:05.165
So I grew up in a small town in Ohio, and
actually, my

00:05:05.165 --> 00:05:12.354
parents and my wife are here, who are from
that same town.

00:05:12.354 --> 00:05:12.611
And-
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:05:12.611 --> 00:05:14.416
&gt;&gt; I talk about my wife all the time in
class.

00:05:14.416 --> 00:05:17.254
And she's actually not Snuffleupagus, she
is real and she's here.

00:05:17.254 --> 00:05:18.581
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:05:18.581 --> 00:05:24.923
&gt;&gt; And anyway, so I grew up in this small
town and I had a lot of marble around me.

00:05:24.923 --> 00:05:28.058
I would have described myself as having

00:05:28.058 --> 00:05:34.624
pretty average abilities, athletic
abilities, average intellect, really.

00:05:34.624 --> 00:05:37.946
And I would say the town I was in was
average [LAUGH].

00:05:37.946 --> 00:05:39.940
And that was my identity.

00:05:39.940 --> 00:05:44.488
And so I had these pretty firm stone
walls.

00:05:44.488 --> 00:05:46.928
And then when I was about 13, like a lot
of us,

00:05:46.928 --> 00:05:50.229
I wanted to feel like I mattered, I wanted
to feel significant.

00:05:50.229 --> 00:05:55.073
And so, in Ohio, one way to do that was
through athletics.

00:05:55.073 --> 00:05:58.257
So I tried out for basketball and I got
cut,

00:05:58.257 --> 00:06:01.809
and I tried out for football and I was too
small.

00:06:01.809 --> 00:06:04.778
So we had wrestling in Ohio, that was one
of the sports we had.

00:06:04.778 --> 00:06:07.598
And wrestling could be any weight class,
and they

00:06:07.598 --> 00:06:10.703
didn't have cuts, so I was like, all
right, great.

00:06:10.703 --> 00:06:14.623
My first three years, seventh and eighth
and ninth grade, really

00:06:14.623 --> 00:06:18.405
the only way to describe my career would
have been forgettable.

00:06:18.405 --> 00:06:24.233
And that, by the way, is a very optimistic
description of those three years.

00:06:24.233 --> 00:06:27.245
So I got to ninth grade that summer, and I
said

00:06:27.245 --> 00:06:31.300
two words that I've been saying my whole
life to that point.

00:06:31.300 --> 00:06:35.667
I said, not me, not now.

00:06:35.667 --> 00:06:36.883
No, I'm not the right person.

00:06:36.883 --> 00:06:38.309
Now's not the right time.

00:06:38.309 --> 00:06:42.033
I go on walks with some of you, and you'll
tell me about something

00:06:42.033 --> 00:06:46.385
you're excited about and I'll hear you say
those words, not me, not now.

00:06:46.385 --> 00:06:48.636
I'm not ready.

00:06:48.636 --> 00:06:52.129
That summer, though, two things happened
for me.

00:06:52.129 --> 00:06:56.086
One was, I bought a Sony Walkman.

00:06:56.086 --> 00:06:59.285
And this was the 1980s version of your
iPhone.

00:06:59.285 --> 00:07:02.478
And this was magical, just as magical as
the iPhone was.

00:07:02.478 --> 00:07:06.150
And I was at the library one time,
checking out some music,

00:07:06.150 --> 00:07:09.909
and there was this program and it said,
Think and Grow Rich.

00:07:09.909 --> 00:07:11.394
I was like, awesome.

00:07:11.394 --> 00:07:13.875
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:07:13.875 --> 00:07:15.762
&gt;&gt; Who doesn't want to think and grow
rich?

00:07:15.762 --> 00:07:16.691
Turns out that book wasn't at all about
thinking and growing rich,

00:07:16.691 --> 00:07:17.781
it was about conquering your inner self,
that's what it's actually about.

00:07:17.781 --> 00:07:22.319
It's a great book, Napoleon Hill.

00:07:22.319 --> 00:07:27.972
But I became addicted to these self help,
for lack of a better word, audio tapes.

00:07:27.972 --> 00:07:33.154
Listened to Zig Ziglar, Tony Robbins, Dale
Carnegie, Stephen Covey.

00:07:33.154 --> 00:07:35.022
So I had a job over the summer, that

00:07:35.022 --> 00:07:38.062
summer, every summer in Ohio, which is
mowing lawns.

00:07:38.062 --> 00:07:41.258
I attach this Walkman and I put in Tony
Robbins, and then

00:07:41.258 --> 00:07:45.489
mowing lawns is pretty straightforward,
you walk in a line, in the sun.

00:07:45.489 --> 00:07:48.339
And then when you're done, you get to the
end

00:07:48.339 --> 00:07:51.265
of the row, you turn around and you walk
back.

00:07:51.265 --> 00:07:55.187
That's how pretty much I just taught you
how to mow a lawn.

00:07:55.187 --> 00:07:58.061
And I did this for six hours a weekend for
my

00:07:58.061 --> 00:08:03.500
entire high school ,and I brainwashed
myself with these tapes, basically.

00:08:03.500 --> 00:08:08.669
And my parents had just gone through a
divorce, and And I wasn't

00:08:08.669 --> 00:08:10.065
really part of a friend group, and

00:08:10.065 --> 00:08:11.954
athletics wasn't really working out for
me.

00:08:11.954 --> 00:08:15.550
So this became something for me to grab on
to, I was really impressionable.

00:08:15.550 --> 00:08:17.350
And I grabbed on to these with everything
I had.

00:08:18.420 --> 00:08:20.950
So I'll summarize, literally, I added up
the hours

00:08:20.950 --> 00:08:23.439
one time and it's 1000s of hours I
listened to.

00:08:23.439 --> 00:08:28.635
I'm going to summarize it for you in about
two minutes, you're welcome.

00:08:28.635 --> 00:08:32.280
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] And the basic summary is that

00:08:32.280 --> 00:08:39.105
about 99% of people never actually figure
out what they want.

00:08:39.105 --> 00:08:41.916
There's a lot of activity, they spend all
this time.

00:08:41.916 --> 00:08:45.310
They're running around, they're busy,
they're going all these directions.

00:08:45.310 --> 00:08:50.152
But they actually never take the time to
figure out, what is it that they want?

00:08:50.152 --> 00:08:52.032
And they said, you will be at the top 1%
if

00:08:52.032 --> 00:08:54.560
you could just figure out what you want,
take that time.

00:08:55.590 --> 00:08:57.260
We're going to talk about that today.

00:08:58.780 --> 00:09:01.478
The best exercise that I know to figure
out what

00:09:01.478 --> 00:09:04.256
you want, I've done with many of you in
class.

00:09:04.256 --> 00:09:06.770
But I'm sorry I'm going to do it again,
because this is my last lecture.

00:09:06.770 --> 00:09:10.200
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Is imagine the following.

00:09:11.350 --> 00:09:15.971
Imagine you rub a magic lamp, and a genie
comes out.

00:09:15.971 --> 00:09:19.760
And the genie says to you, I can't give
you any wish.

00:09:19.760 --> 00:09:24.098
But what I can give you is that whatever
you throw yourself into with your

00:09:24.098 --> 00:09:27.062
life, whatever you really commit yourself
to,

00:09:27.062 --> 00:09:29.761
that that is going to work out amazingly
well.

00:09:29.761 --> 00:09:30.986
It's going to be way harder than you

00:09:30.986 --> 00:09:32.792
think, it's going to take longer than you
think.

00:09:32.792 --> 00:09:36.488
You're going to have [SOUND] some face
plants, it's going to be tough.

00:09:36.488 --> 00:09:41.243
And when you fail, you're going to think
that I have abandoned you, but I haven't.

00:09:41.243 --> 00:09:43.424
But it's going to be the journey of a
lifetime.

00:09:43.424 --> 00:09:45.440
What would you wish for from that genie if
you

00:09:45.440 --> 00:09:48.069
really had that power, if you really had
that blessing?

00:09:48.069 --> 00:09:50.146
That's my favorite prompt that I've come
up with,

00:09:50.146 --> 00:09:52.325
and I've shared that, obviously, with many
of you.

00:09:54.182 --> 00:09:56.892
The second thing that happened that summer

00:09:56.892 --> 00:09:59.835
was there was this movie called Vision
Quest.

00:09:59.835 --> 00:10:01.726
How many of you seen Vision Quest?

00:10:01.726 --> 00:10:02.946
Like two of you, awesome.

00:10:02.946 --> 00:10:04.330
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:10:04.330 --> 00:10:05.684
&gt;&gt; Actually and my wife, too, I've made

00:10:05.684 --> 00:10:07.210
her see it many times, she raised her
hand.

00:10:08.950 --> 00:10:10.714
Vision Quest [LAUGH] is, so for those of

00:10:10.714 --> 00:10:13.320
you don't know, can't believe you haven't
seen it.

00:10:13.320 --> 00:10:17.640
Vision Quest is a 1980s movie, it's kind
of like Rocky for wrestling.

00:10:17.640 --> 00:10:19.062
That's the best way to describe it.

00:10:19.062 --> 00:10:21.900
There's this wrestler named Louden Swain.

00:10:21.900 --> 00:10:24.957
And he decides he's going to cut down two
weight classes,

00:10:24.957 --> 00:10:28.740
and he's going to wrestle this defending
state champion Brian Shute.

00:10:28.740 --> 00:10:32.344
And his buddy, kind of his friend that
goes on this journey with

00:10:32.344 --> 00:10:36.900
him, says, Louden, you're on a vision
quest, this is a vision quest.

00:10:36.900 --> 00:10:40.682
Well, this is a B movie, probably, by any
standard, except if you were a

00:10:40.682 --> 00:10:43.244
15 year old wannabe wrestler from
Perrysburg,

00:10:43.244 --> 00:10:45.900
Ohio, in which case it was my anthem.

00:10:45.900 --> 00:10:48.816
And I still to this day, I've seen that
movie more than any other

00:10:48.816 --> 00:10:51.950
movie I've ever seen, I can say pretty
much every line in the movie.

00:10:52.990 --> 00:10:55.973
So anyway, those two things conspired, and
I

00:10:55.973 --> 00:10:58.661
go back to wrestling my tenth grade year.

00:10:58.661 --> 00:11:04.111
And I'm standing in the wrestling room,
and I'm looking up at the wall.

00:11:04.111 --> 00:11:06.960
We're having a pizza party in the fall.

00:11:06.960 --> 00:11:08.907
And I'm looking up at the wall, and
there's a list

00:11:08.907 --> 00:11:11.830
of all the starting wrestlers, and I'm not
one of them.

00:11:11.830 --> 00:11:16.033
And my buddy comes up, and he puts his arm
around me.

00:11:16.033 --> 00:11:19.043
And he says, Weaver, only place you can
make

00:11:19.043 --> 00:11:22.761
that weight, make the starting lineup, is
at 125 pounds.

00:11:22.761 --> 00:11:26.940
And he punches me in the gut, literally
and figuratively punches me in the gut.

00:11:26.940 --> 00:11:31.445
So to give you an idea, well, anyway, so I
look

00:11:31.445 --> 00:11:36.415
at this, and I decide that I have a
decision to make.

00:11:36.415 --> 00:11:40.786
And I'm eating this piece of pizza, and I
throw the pizza in this trash.

00:11:40.786 --> 00:11:43.306
And I had this ceremonious moment where I
said,

00:11:43.306 --> 00:11:45.892
all right, I'm going to go on my own
vision quest.

00:11:45.892 --> 00:11:49.100
I'm going to drop to 125 pounds, make the
starting lineup.

00:11:50.200 --> 00:11:53.420
Now this story is not end the way that
Vision Quest does.

00:11:53.420 --> 00:11:57.550
I do make the weight, by the way, just
give you a sense of 125 pounds.

00:11:57.550 --> 00:12:00.973
I was at the same height as I am now, I
weigh 185 right now.

00:12:00.973 --> 00:12:05.610
So when I decided to cut the weight I was
150, and then another 25 pounds from that.

00:12:05.610 --> 00:12:08.070
So it was, to this day, one of the hardest
things I've ever done.

00:12:09.570 --> 00:12:12.175
But that moment when I threw the piece of
pizza in the

00:12:12.175 --> 00:12:15.960
trash would turn out to be the turning
point in my life.

00:12:15.960 --> 00:12:20.410
And the reason is that I basically drew a
line.

00:12:20.410 --> 00:12:23.710
I didn't do this figuratively, but I did
this kind of in my head.

00:12:23.710 --> 00:12:25.670
I drew this line here.

00:12:25.670 --> 00:12:27.800
And this line, on this side of the line is
where I was.

00:12:27.800 --> 00:12:30.792
And on the other side of the line are the
things that I

00:12:30.792 --> 00:12:34.494
really wanted, the thing I wanted to be,
the goals that I had.

00:12:34.494 --> 00:12:38.203
And up till that point in my life, I'd
always been tiptoeing

00:12:38.203 --> 00:12:41.417
and putting my toe over on the other side
of the line.

00:12:41.417 --> 00:12:43.564
But I really never believed it, I never

00:12:43.564 --> 00:12:46.502
committed, I never stepped fully on the
other side.

00:12:46.502 --> 00:12:48.292
And that was the first time in my life
where

00:12:48.292 --> 00:12:50.391
I knew when I threw that piece of pizza
away, that

00:12:50.391 --> 00:12:52.540
this wasn't going to be something I was
going to do for a

00:12:52.540 --> 00:12:54.920
week, it wasn't something I was going to
do for a month.

00:12:54.920 --> 00:12:58.922
This is something that I was committed and
I was going all

00:12:58.922 --> 00:13:02.426
in and I did, and my mom can attest to
that [LAUGH].

00:13:02.426 --> 00:13:03.750
So there are two things.

00:13:05.700 --> 00:13:08.661
My story today, I'm going to share five
things with you,

00:13:08.661 --> 00:13:11.502
five ways that you can free yourself from
the marble.

00:13:11.502 --> 00:13:15.850
And I uncovered two of them that I'll
kind of highlight for you in that story.

00:13:17.090 --> 00:13:22.766
The first one is that these walls that we
have around us, we view them as stone,

00:13:22.766 --> 00:13:28.444
and they're the most dangerous when we
don't even realize that we have them.

00:13:28.444 --> 00:13:31.415
And those are the stories we tell
ourselves, the assumptions we

00:13:31.415 --> 00:13:33.733
make, the things that we say we can and
can't do.

00:13:33.733 --> 00:13:37.168
The most constraining walls that you will
have are the

00:13:37.168 --> 00:13:40.382
ones that you have made in your mind for
yourself.

00:13:40.382 --> 00:13:45.621
Those are the ones that will cause you the
most problems.

00:13:45.621 --> 00:13:48.858
And those walls are not actually made of

00:13:48.858 --> 00:13:53.185
stone, but if you push them, they're
malleable.

00:13:53.185 --> 00:13:58.246
And one way to push them hard is to
commit, to step over and not look back.

00:13:58.246 --> 00:14:00.369
And then those walls will start to give,
and they'll

00:14:00.369 --> 00:14:03.000
start to yield, and you can push, and
they'll move.

00:14:03.000 --> 00:14:06.721
And so when I pushed on this wall, first,
I had a wall that was my athletic

00:14:06.721 --> 00:14:09.222
ability, I have a wall of intelligence, my
wall

00:14:09.222 --> 00:14:11.239
of who I fit in, my friends, all that.

00:14:11.239 --> 00:14:15.529
But I started pushing on this athletic
wall, and it moved, and it gave.

00:14:15.529 --> 00:14:18.341
And eventually I cleared it out
altogether, and had streaks, and I

00:14:18.341 --> 00:14:22.420
became captain of a national championship
rowing team, having never rowed before.

00:14:22.420 --> 00:14:24.437
And I realized there was no wall there at
all.

00:14:25.721 --> 00:14:27.312
It was just walls I was erecting.

00:14:27.312 --> 00:14:29.730
And the same was true with my view of my
identity for

00:14:29.730 --> 00:14:34.360
intelligence, and many other things, is
that they weren't walls at all.

00:14:34.360 --> 00:14:37.882
So my first takeaway is, almost any wall
that you can imagine will

00:14:37.882 --> 00:14:42.100
yield to you with time, it will yield to
you when you're committed.

00:14:42.100 --> 00:14:45.270
It will yield to you at your best, there's
almost no wall that won't yield.

00:14:46.520 --> 00:14:49.420
Second takeaway I have has to do with your
identity.

00:14:51.310 --> 00:14:54.230
We all act consistent with our identity.

00:14:55.940 --> 00:15:01.070
So we're going to behave today based on
how we perceive ourselves.

00:15:01.070 --> 00:15:02.377
What is our identity?

00:15:02.377 --> 00:15:03.829
So where's your identity come from?

00:15:05.760 --> 00:15:09.305
Well, your identity comes from your
subconscious mind.

00:15:09.305 --> 00:15:11.055
And your subconscious, 95% of all the

00:15:11.055 --> 00:15:13.110
things you think are in your subconscious
mind.

00:15:14.430 --> 00:15:16.957
And so your subconscious is really
powerful.

00:15:16.957 --> 00:15:23.050
Another thing I learned from these tapes,
95% is your subconscious mind.

00:15:24.090 --> 00:15:27.126
And you've heard the expression, we only
use a fraction of our minds.

00:15:27.126 --> 00:15:28.580
How many of you've heard that expression,
right?

00:15:28.580 --> 00:15:29.730
We only use a little bit of it.

00:15:29.730 --> 00:15:30.860
So I would say it differently.

00:15:30.860 --> 00:15:33.356
I would say only a small fraction of our
mind is

00:15:33.356 --> 00:15:36.980
working for us, and the other 95%, it's
all over the place.

00:15:36.980 --> 00:15:40.010
And often, more often than not, it's
working against us.

00:15:40.010 --> 00:15:41.560
So our identity comes from when our

00:15:41.560 --> 00:15:43.648
subconscious is picking up cues all the
time.

00:15:43.648 --> 00:15:46.686
It says like, you're not that good at
this, you're not that good at that.

00:15:46.686 --> 00:15:48.670
You're not comparing well to this person.

00:15:48.670 --> 00:15:50.218
Man, I failed at this.

00:15:50.218 --> 00:15:53.150
I'm not as good as this, that's our
identity is coming.

00:15:53.150 --> 00:15:55.020
So really our identity is coming from our
past.

00:15:56.802 --> 00:15:59.831
What happened is, I started developing
these

00:15:59.831 --> 00:16:03.489
genie goals, and having something I
actually wanted.

00:16:03.489 --> 00:16:05.450
And one of the things these tapes would
say is

00:16:05.450 --> 00:16:08.398
visualize it, write it down, talk about it
all the time.

00:16:08.398 --> 00:16:13.517
In a very short period of time, my
identity started becoming my destination.

00:16:13.517 --> 00:16:15.701
It'll happen like that.

00:16:15.701 --> 00:16:19.178
So if you want to free yourself from the
marble, consider having

00:16:19.178 --> 00:16:22.604
your identity be where you want to go, not
where you've been.

00:16:22.604 --> 00:16:25.939
It'll completely change the way you look
at the world.

00:16:28.640 --> 00:16:31.218
I want to go back to the movie, Vision
Quest, for a second.

00:16:31.218 --> 00:16:39.042
So Vision Quest follows the arc of a very
common architecture of stories.

00:16:39.042 --> 00:16:45.109
Joseph Campbell identified this arc, and
he called it the hero's journey.

00:16:45.109 --> 00:16:47.743
So the hero's journey goes something like
this.

00:16:47.743 --> 00:16:51.110
It says, once upon a time, there was a

00:16:51.110 --> 00:16:55.547
hero and that hero felt something inside
of them.

00:16:55.547 --> 00:17:00.428
They felt that they had this power that
they were ready to give to the world.

00:17:00.428 --> 00:17:02.389
And yet, this hero lived in this ordinary
world

00:17:02.389 --> 00:17:04.899
and they'd experienced the power at times,
but not fully.

00:17:04.899 --> 00:17:09.585
They weren't really 100% able to express
this.

00:17:09.585 --> 00:17:12.497
And then the story goes on and says, and
then

00:17:12.497 --> 00:17:16.449
one day, until one day, something changed
for that hero.

00:17:16.449 --> 00:17:21.410
They heard some call to adventure, some
call to action.

00:17:21.410 --> 00:17:23.010
And the hero's reluctant at first.

00:17:23.010 --> 00:17:25.927
They're not sure if they should answer
this call.

00:17:25.927 --> 00:17:29.886
And I want to argue, this is where many of
you are right now.

00:17:29.886 --> 00:17:33.795
Make no mistake, you are the hero.

00:17:33.795 --> 00:17:38.153
You have this power inside of you that
you've felt that you know is there.

00:17:38.153 --> 00:17:41.306
You've had this power that you've
expressed at times

00:17:41.306 --> 00:17:44.057
in your life that is incredible and you
felt it.

00:17:44.057 --> 00:17:49.006
And I know from teaching you, having
lunches, coffees,

00:17:49.006 --> 00:17:54.566
dinners, drinks, I know that each of you
also is hearing a call.

00:17:54.566 --> 00:17:58.493
The call could be that there's a problem
that breaks

00:17:58.493 --> 00:18:02.340
your heart that you want to be a part of
the solution.

00:18:02.340 --> 00:18:04.556
The call could be that there's something
you want to introduce

00:18:04.556 --> 00:18:06.820
to the world of product and technology,
something like that.

00:18:06.820 --> 00:18:10.140
The call, it could be something more
subtle.

00:18:10.140 --> 00:18:12.150
The call is your call.

00:18:12.150 --> 00:18:13.690
It's not what I think, it's not what your
neighbor

00:18:13.690 --> 00:18:15.760
thinks, it's not what your roommate
thinks, it's what you think.

00:18:15.760 --> 00:18:18.434
Your call could be that you want to be a
teacher, the call could

00:18:18.434 --> 00:18:20.963
be you want to be an actor, or a writer,
or whatever it is.

00:18:20.963 --> 00:18:23.101
Sometimes for some of you, it's really
loud,

00:18:23.101 --> 00:18:24.922
that call, you know exactly what it is.

00:18:24.922 --> 00:18:26.462
And for some of you, it's a whisper.

00:18:26.462 --> 00:18:30.929
But you gotta listen to that call, you
gotta hear that.

00:18:30.929 --> 00:18:34.842
And many of you are reluctant right now,
you're in the reluctant

00:18:34.842 --> 00:18:38.558
hero part of the story, at least from
spending time with you.

00:18:38.558 --> 00:18:41.492
I was the reluctant hero too when I
graduated.

00:18:41.492 --> 00:18:42.920
If you were to ask me any time in the

00:18:42.920 --> 00:18:44.910
two years I was at Stanford, what are you
going to

00:18:44.910 --> 00:18:46.900
do, I said, I'm going to go build a firm
that's

00:18:46.900 --> 00:18:49.427
going to unleash people and is going to be
built around talent.

00:18:49.427 --> 00:18:50.687
That's what I said I was going to do.

00:18:50.687 --> 00:18:53.876
And then when it came time to graduate, I
listened to

00:18:53.876 --> 00:18:57.208
all my fears and my doubts and my limiting
beliefs, and

00:18:57.208 --> 00:19:00.034
I kind of took this, I had this decision
to make

00:19:00.034 --> 00:19:03.601
between these two jobs or doing what I
really wanted to do.

00:19:03.601 --> 00:19:08.019
I made the decision the way a lot of you
make a decision, which is like this.

00:19:08.019 --> 00:19:11.259
I made it in my head and I said, well,
here's a list of the pros,

00:19:11.259 --> 00:19:13.119
and here's the list of the cons, and

00:19:13.119 --> 00:19:15.896
here's the good things, and here's the bad
things.

00:19:15.896 --> 00:19:18.231
I even had a spreadsheet of expected
value.

00:19:18.231 --> 00:19:20.152
And this is the way I know you make
decisions because I

00:19:20.152 --> 00:19:22.378
talked to you about it, and you all
kind of go like this.

00:19:22.378 --> 00:19:26.733
And then I asked 37 different people for
advice,

00:19:26.733 --> 00:19:31.197
thinking the 37th was going to have my
answer for me.

00:19:31.197 --> 00:19:33.581
I'm usually the 37th on your list too, by
the way.

00:19:33.581 --> 00:19:38.153
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; So I made this decision.

00:19:38.153 --> 00:19:43.850
And then, of course, with all this stuff,
I went to the well lit, well worn

00:19:43.850 --> 00:19:47.502
path, because that seemed more logical and
more

00:19:47.502 --> 00:19:52.334
straightforward, and that's where all that
analysis led me.

00:19:52.334 --> 00:19:57.752
I went to a firm, and when I showed up,
honestly, a little bit of me died.

00:19:57.752 --> 00:20:02.275
[LAUGH] And a little bit of me died each
day I was there, until.

00:20:02.275 --> 00:20:06.359
So I kind of was that here looking out the
window, and I'm kind of

00:20:06.359 --> 00:20:10.925
like waiting for someone to come rescue me
and take me on this journey.

00:20:10.925 --> 00:20:12.410
But after a while, I stopped looking out

00:20:12.410 --> 00:20:14.080
the window and I just became my
environment.

00:20:14.080 --> 00:20:19.196
Their values became my values, their goals
became my goals.

00:20:19.196 --> 00:20:24.816
Once again in my life, I had said, not me,
not now, not me, not now.

00:20:24.816 --> 00:20:30.686
I had another catalyst come in the form of
sort of a

00:20:30.686 --> 00:20:36.996
very tragic event, which is a close friend
of mine.

00:20:36.996 --> 00:20:39.685
He woke up like all of us did today
thinking he had

00:20:39.685 --> 00:20:42.926
his whole life in front of him, and that
was his last day.

00:20:42.926 --> 00:20:47.793
And I realized that I don't know what I
was

00:20:47.793 --> 00:20:53.229
putting off, I don't know when the time
was.

00:20:53.229 --> 00:20:55.421
If it's not now, then when's it going to
be?

00:20:55.421 --> 00:20:59.966
And I told myself at that time I was never
going to say the words not

00:20:59.966 --> 00:21:04.959
me, not now again, and I don't know that I
have said it since then.

00:21:04.959 --> 00:21:07.285
The third thing to think about in

00:21:07.285 --> 00:21:11.422
unleashing yourself from the marble is
there's another

00:21:11.422 --> 00:21:16.863
way to make a decision, which is not in
your head, but it's in your heart.

00:21:16.863 --> 00:21:20.844
And there's a quote, which is, you can't

00:21:20.844 --> 00:21:24.943
solve the problems of the mind with the
mind.

00:21:24.943 --> 00:21:28.774
You can't solve your mind with your mind.

00:21:28.774 --> 00:21:31.634
But you can, your answer is most likely in
your heart.

00:21:31.634 --> 00:21:34.775
So the way to make that decision is to
really

00:21:34.775 --> 00:21:38.976
silence your mind, and just think what's
true for you.

00:21:38.976 --> 00:21:40.155
What's real?

00:21:40.155 --> 00:21:44.372
Give yourself space, time.

00:21:44.372 --> 00:21:47.507
What's meaningful for you?

00:21:47.507 --> 00:21:48.722
What's the impact you want to have?

00:21:48.722 --> 00:21:53.312
What would you do if you knew you weren't
going to fail?

00:21:53.312 --> 00:21:57.331
And give yourself the space to think about
that.

00:21:57.331 --> 00:21:59.324
And my guess is you already know

00:21:59.324 --> 00:22:03.320
your answer, you're just not giving
yourself permission.

00:22:03.320 --> 00:22:06.306
So the third thing is make your decisions
with

00:22:06.306 --> 00:22:10.403
your heart and not your head, and give
yourself permission.

00:22:10.403 --> 00:22:13.957
Nobody's coming to your rescue.

00:22:13.957 --> 00:22:17.416
I thought I'd talk quickly about the
journey itself.

00:22:17.416 --> 00:22:20.081
Some of you have started your journey,
some of you are going to start later.

00:22:20.081 --> 00:22:21.814
Some of your journeys are completely
different

00:22:21.814 --> 00:22:23.156
than my journey, but I'll tell you a

00:22:23.156 --> 00:22:24.500
little bit about my journey because it's

00:22:24.500 --> 00:22:26.162
going to have some things in common with
yours.

00:22:26.162 --> 00:22:31.102
I started my firm, and it took me a year
to raise a fund.

00:22:31.102 --> 00:22:33.994
We raised way less than I thought, it took
way longer

00:22:33.994 --> 00:22:37.227
than I thought, but we got going, so we're
in business.

00:22:37.227 --> 00:22:39.922
This was the first time I was going to buy
companies not using

00:22:39.922 --> 00:22:43.193
credit card debt, but I actually had some
real money and investors.

00:22:43.193 --> 00:22:47.030
I was excited, and then promptly lost
money on five of

00:22:47.030 --> 00:22:50.966
my first eight deals, and lost money on my
first fund.

00:22:50.966 --> 00:22:56.643
I had probably ten classmates invested in
my fund that lost them money.

00:22:56.643 --> 00:22:57.950
I was embarrassed.

00:22:57.950 --> 00:23:02.071
At my fifth reunion, I had a friend put
his arm around me and say, Graham, during

00:23:02.071 --> 00:23:04.677
the greatest wealth creation of all time,
this was

00:23:04.677 --> 00:23:08.144
the dotcom era, you're buying stupid label
printing companies.

00:23:08.144 --> 00:23:11.554
[LAUGH] I remember thinking, yeah, that's
pretty much accurate.

00:23:11.554 --> 00:23:13.301
[LAUGH] Not only am I buying stupid label

00:23:13.301 --> 00:23:15.930
printing companies, it's not really going
that well.

00:23:15.930 --> 00:23:20.156
So it wasn't good, but we started clawing
our way back and then

00:23:20.156 --> 00:23:25.116
we got a little better, and then bam, we
got flattened by the recession.

00:23:25.116 --> 00:23:28.510
I didn't raise a fund for three years, and
for

00:23:28.510 --> 00:23:33.310
the second time, I drained my savings,
keeping payroll going.

00:23:33.310 --> 00:23:36.440
So this is my inspiring entrepreneurship
story.

00:23:36.440 --> 00:23:43.301
But the point of the story is that every
journey, using the hero's journey analogy,

00:23:43.301 --> 00:23:49.606
think of Yoda in Star Wars or Princess
Bride, the journey ends up in the swamp.

00:23:49.606 --> 00:23:52.225
You will end up in the swamp at some
point.

00:23:52.225 --> 00:23:56.149
And the swamp is where you're mucking
around in the mud,

00:23:56.149 --> 00:23:59.437
and you're not sure how to get out of the
swamp.

00:23:59.437 --> 00:24:02.684
You don't know if you're going to get out
of the

00:24:02.684 --> 00:24:06.283
swamp or when, and you feel like you have
failed.

00:24:06.283 --> 00:24:09.780
This swamp feels like failure, is what it
feels like.

00:24:11.480 --> 00:24:14.080
But, I want to just talk about failure for
one second.

00:24:14.080 --> 00:24:17.468
I'm going to tell you that none of you
fear failure, and

00:24:17.468 --> 00:24:21.708
you're going to be like, no, Graham, I
pretty much fear failure.

00:24:21.708 --> 00:24:26.075
But you don't, what you fear is underneath

00:24:26.075 --> 00:24:30.805
failure, which is you fear what happens
next.

00:24:30.805 --> 00:24:33.926
You fear that you can't handle failure,
that whatever comes

00:24:33.926 --> 00:24:36.577
after that, you're not going to be able to
handle it.

00:24:36.577 --> 00:24:39.763
But then if you think back to your lives,
how many of you

00:24:39.763 --> 00:24:44.492
have had something go really wrong that
you would have categorized as a failure?

00:24:44.492 --> 00:24:48.254
Every single one of you, and you know what
happened?

00:24:48.254 --> 00:24:51.014
You got up, you kept going, you're here,
and you're

00:24:51.014 --> 00:24:54.650
about to graduate from Stanford Business
School [LAUGH] no less.

00:24:54.650 --> 00:24:57.862
So, you can handle failure, every single
person

00:24:57.862 --> 00:25:01.313
can handle failure and that's really what
you fear.

00:25:01.313 --> 00:25:05.955
So in this swamp, this one quote is the
thing that kind

00:25:05.955 --> 00:25:11.025
of helped me a lot is this quote by Vince
Lombardi who said.

00:25:11.025 --> 00:25:12.775
He was the coach of the Green Bay Packers

00:25:12.775 --> 00:25:14.949
and he said, the Green Bay Packers never
lost the

00:25:14.949 --> 00:25:17.071
football game, but there were a couple of
cases

00:25:17.071 --> 00:25:19.054
where we ran out of time, I love that
quote.

00:25:19.054 --> 00:25:22.551
But the best thing about the swamp is
you're in control of the most

00:25:22.551 --> 00:25:27.017
powerful part, the most powerful tool that
you need, which is you have the clock.

00:25:27.017 --> 00:25:29.332
You're the one who gets to call the game,

00:25:29.332 --> 00:25:32.320
and you don't have to call it until you're
winning.

00:25:32.320 --> 00:25:35.972
So, my fourth takeaway is, the journey
you'll be on

00:25:35.972 --> 00:25:40.694
will go through the swamp, and just
recognize that that's normal.

00:25:40.694 --> 00:25:44.112
When you're in the swamp realize everyone
else has been in

00:25:44.112 --> 00:25:48.940
the swamp too, realize failure is not
fatal, failure is not final.

00:25:48.940 --> 00:25:53.500
You can handle failure, and ultimately you
control the clock.

00:25:53.500 --> 00:25:56.707
As Winston Churchill says, if you're going
through

00:25:56.707 --> 00:26:00.070
hell, keep going, [LAUGH] so that's my
four takeaway.

00:26:00.070 --> 00:26:02.710
So, I'll summarize my takeaways and then
I'll leave you with one final thought.

00:26:04.260 --> 00:26:09.547
First one is, the walls that are erected
around you, the most constraining

00:26:09.547 --> 00:26:15.060
ones are the ones you put on yourself, and
that those walls will move.

00:26:15.060 --> 00:26:17.343
Those walls are not a stone at all,
they're

00:26:17.343 --> 00:26:19.873
very, very malleable, but they will move
if you

00:26:19.873 --> 00:26:22.774
throw yourself in with commitment, and any
will almost

00:26:22.774 --> 00:26:24.828
any wall will yield to you at your best.

00:26:24.828 --> 00:26:29.922
Second is, consider your identity, you
will behave consistent with your identity.

00:26:29.922 --> 00:26:33.700
So, start to think about your identity as
your destination not your past.

00:26:35.170 --> 00:26:38.026
Third, make your decisions from your
heart, and 50

00:26:38.026 --> 00:26:41.710
years I've never made a bad decision from
my heart.

00:26:41.710 --> 00:26:44.002
Make your decisions from your heart and

00:26:44.002 --> 00:26:46.923
not your head, nobody's coming to your
rescue.

00:26:46.923 --> 00:26:49.238
No one's going to make that decision for
you, no one's going to

00:26:49.238 --> 00:26:51.522
tell you it's time, no one's going to tell
you that ready.

00:26:51.522 --> 00:26:57.000
You're the only one who can give yourself
permission, just you, but guess what?

00:26:57.000 --> 00:27:00.277
You're the only one who needs to give you
permission too.

00:27:00.277 --> 00:27:03.586
And remember, the journey will go through
the swamp, when you're in

00:27:03.586 --> 00:27:06.407
the swamp, give me a call, I'll help you
out of the swamp.

00:27:06.407 --> 00:27:10.235
But it's going to be normal and failure is
not

00:27:10.235 --> 00:27:15.136
fatal, failure is not final, you control
the clock.

00:27:15.136 --> 00:27:24.760
So, I'll just end with a story, my story's
about executive coach named Tom.

00:27:24.760 --> 00:27:27.424
So, I'm in the middle of the swamp, waist

00:27:27.424 --> 00:27:31.500
deep, this is right around the time of the
recession.

00:27:31.500 --> 00:27:34.226
And I hire this coach named Tom, so here's

00:27:34.226 --> 00:27:37.493
my introduction to Tom I'm going to
reenact it here.

00:27:37.493 --> 00:27:43.580
I'm like, hey, Tom, I'm Graham, nice to
meet you, Tom gives me this blank look.

00:27:43.580 --> 00:27:47.982
Like Graham I always say this, because
people Graham's kind of weird obviously,

00:27:47.982 --> 00:27:52.016
Graham like Graham Cracker [LAUGH], so, I
just made it's a nervous thing.

00:27:52.016 --> 00:27:58.052
This is Tom, Tom says, Graham, what if you
suddenly

00:27:58.052 --> 00:28:04.909
realized you were put on this earth to do
something special?

00:28:04.909 --> 00:28:06.339
So, Tom, right?

00:28:06.339 --> 00:28:12.510
[LAUGH] So, good to meet you [LAUGH], Tom,
that was literally our introduction.

00:28:12.510 --> 00:28:15.329
And then, we talked for two years and
every single

00:28:15.329 --> 00:28:19.370
meeting we had, at some point, met
sometimes multiple points crale.

00:28:20.620 --> 00:28:26.299
What if you suddenly realized you were put
on this earth to do something special?

00:28:26.299 --> 00:28:31.007
And this one time we were at in San
Francisco, we're at the Deli and I'm

00:28:31.007 --> 00:28:33.834
like, there's three people in front of us

00:28:33.834 --> 00:28:37.226
and I'm on my phone, Tom's like, Graham
[LAUGH].

00:28:37.226 --> 00:28:43.915
What if you suddenly realize you were put
on this earth to do something special?

00:28:43.915 --> 00:28:47.931
So, I'll ask all of you, what if you
suddenly realized

00:28:47.931 --> 00:28:52.131
you were put on this earth to do something
really special?

00:28:53.848 --> 00:28:57.750
Think about that, and I want to draw a
line for you.

00:28:57.750 --> 00:28:59.951
And you can do this tonight at home or you

00:28:59.951 --> 00:29:03.040
can when you have some time, but there's a
line here.

00:29:03.040 --> 00:29:08.330
And you're on this side of the line, on
this side of line it's really familiar.

00:29:08.330 --> 00:29:10.715
You've been here before, you have these
thoughts from the past,

00:29:10.715 --> 00:29:13.459
you have an identity, you're very
comfortable in this area, right here.

00:29:15.160 --> 00:29:16.823
In this state of mind you probably have
some

00:29:16.823 --> 00:29:19.600
walls, you're in your head, you're making
analytical decisions.

00:29:20.630 --> 00:29:24.320
But there's a line, and over here is
really what

00:29:24.320 --> 00:29:28.730
you want, on the other side, it's what you
really want.

00:29:28.730 --> 00:29:32.312
You know that and only you would know that
and your heart knows that.

00:29:32.312 --> 00:29:38.317
And over here is you realizing that the
hero's journey isn't just the

00:29:38.317 --> 00:29:44.550
best architecture of a story, but it's the
architecture of your life.

00:29:44.550 --> 00:29:47.890
It's the best story you could write with
this one life we got, and

00:29:47.890 --> 00:29:51.490
you're the hero of that journey, you get
to be the hero of your story.

00:29:52.860 --> 00:29:56.232
And over here, on the other side of this
line,

00:29:56.232 --> 00:30:01.160
is this call to adventure, and you've been
called to.

00:30:01.160 --> 00:30:07.212
If you really listen, and you really pay
attention, there's a call over here.

00:30:07.212 --> 00:30:12.173
And finally on this side of the line, is
going back to what Michelangelo

00:30:12.173 --> 00:30:17.559
said, that as you realizing that you
already have everything that you need.

00:30:17.559 --> 00:30:21.700
And that you already are everything that

00:30:21.700 --> 00:30:26.951
you need, and that you're enough, sure
enough.

00:30:26.951 --> 00:30:30.329
Well, I finally realized, with Tom I would
go back and

00:30:30.329 --> 00:30:34.780
forth thinking, is he a little crazy or is
he the Messiah?

00:30:34.780 --> 00:30:39.605
Sometimes in the same conversation, and I
finally realized what he was saying.

00:30:39.605 --> 00:30:43.804
He wasn't saying, Tom wasn't asking me if
I

00:30:43.804 --> 00:30:48.121
was put on this earth to do something
special.

00:30:48.121 --> 00:30:53.614
He was asking me, what would happen if I
realized that?

00:30:56.986 --> 00:31:05.151
So class of 2022, what would happen if you
realized that you stepped over this line?

00:31:05.151 --> 00:31:06.303
Thank you.

00:31:06.303 --> 00:31:16.303
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:31:28.508 --> 00:31:30.339
&gt;&gt; Thank you so much Graham.

00:31:30.339 --> 00:31:33.241
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:31:33.241 --> 00:31:36.322
&gt;&gt; Thank you so much Graham for that
wonderful last lecture.

00:31:36.322 --> 00:31:39.698
We're going to move to Q&amp;A, so, please
raise your

00:31:39.698 --> 00:31:42.938
hands if you have a question you would
like to ask.

00:32:01.301 --> 00:32:05.040
&gt;&gt; Chris, I'm a micron but I'll go first,
Zhan, can you hear me?

00:32:05.040 --> 00:32:05.415
&gt;&gt; Yes.

00:32:05.415 --> 00:32:07.532
&gt;&gt; Graham, thanks so much for that, I'm

00:32:07.532 --> 00:32:11.290
always struck in this conversation and
hearing about you.

00:32:11.290 --> 00:32:13.385
Talk about in class, and talking about
these

00:32:13.385 --> 00:32:15.765
limiting beliefs which you talked about
here too.

00:32:15.765 --> 00:32:20.082
Do you ever feel the tension between
convincing yourself that you

00:32:20.082 --> 00:32:25.110
are enough but also keeping that drive to
do something special?

00:32:25.110 --> 00:32:27.150
And how do you navigate that tension?

00:32:27.150 --> 00:32:28.550
&gt;&gt; No, I have it all the time.

00:32:28.550 --> 00:32:32.116
I mean, it seeps in, it's not like it just
goes away,

00:32:32.116 --> 00:32:37.030
you have the doubts and fears and limiting
beliefs all the time.

00:32:37.030 --> 00:32:41.201
And it's kind of what I said, which is I
try to think about what is

00:32:41.201 --> 00:32:46.290
it that I want, what's the bigger why, and
what if I just lean into that?

00:32:46.290 --> 00:32:49.461
And yeah, definitely sometimes I have to
remind myself

00:32:49.461 --> 00:32:52.640
that I'm enough, it doesn't go away, those
voices.

00:32:53.930 --> 00:32:56.405
There's a concept that people have used

00:32:56.405 --> 00:33:00.260
calling our voices inside, our gremlin or
saboteurs.

00:33:00.260 --> 00:33:02.135
And I remember thinking I was the only
person who had

00:33:02.135 --> 00:33:04.410
this voice that was pounding me with all
this negative stuff.

00:33:04.410 --> 00:33:06.568
And then I realized, my gosh, we all have
that.

00:33:06.568 --> 00:33:10.117
[LAUGH] So I think, it doesn't go away,
but I think the awareness

00:33:10.117 --> 00:33:13.629
that it's there is really the best thing
you can do to fight it.

00:33:21.725 --> 00:33:23.477
&gt;&gt; Caroline has the next question.

00:33:23.477 --> 00:33:25.644
&gt;&gt; Kevin.

00:33:25.644 --> 00:33:27.499
&gt;&gt; I also go by Caroline.

00:33:27.499 --> 00:33:29.730
Graham, thanks, appreciate the insights.

00:33:29.730 --> 00:33:32.766
On one hand you talk about this sense of
urgency and

00:33:32.766 --> 00:33:36.393
actively chasing what it is you think
you're called to do.

00:33:36.393 --> 00:33:41.810
On this other hand, there's this notion of
delayed gratification, and spending time

00:33:41.810 --> 00:33:44.738
either getting the credentials, or
garnering

00:33:44.738 --> 00:33:47.606
the experiences before you chase that
thing.

00:33:47.606 --> 00:33:50.318
Do you ever feel tension between those two
things?

00:33:50.318 --> 00:33:52.730
And if so, how do you reconcile it?

00:33:52.730 --> 00:33:55.229
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I don't know that there's a
conflict,

00:33:55.229 --> 00:33:58.240
I think that there's a false dichotomy in
that, Kevin.

00:33:59.760 --> 00:34:02.648
What I mean is, when you're clear on where
you want to

00:34:02.648 --> 00:34:07.750
go, you should 100% accumulate things that
are going to take you there.

00:34:07.750 --> 00:34:10.429
And if that is knowledge, if that's
experience, accumulate

00:34:10.429 --> 00:34:12.558
things that are going to take you where
you want to go.

00:34:14.455 --> 00:34:19.970
That's different than, this is where I
want to go, I'm not ready.

00:34:19.970 --> 00:34:23.290
So instead, I'm going to kind of start on
this journey over here.

00:34:23.290 --> 00:34:25.060
And they're very different.

00:34:25.060 --> 00:34:29.398
So I think what you're describing is,
yeah, sure, if you're super clear on

00:34:29.398 --> 00:34:31.567
your north star, anything you want to do

00:34:31.567 --> 00:34:34.610
to move toward that is going to be
awesome.

00:34:34.610 --> 00:34:38.063
But just be intellectually honest, if
you're really doing that, or

00:34:38.063 --> 00:34:41.176
if you're just kind of moving tangential
to where you want to go.

00:34:44.708 --> 00:34:46.350
&gt;&gt; Another question down here for Holly.

00:34:50.715 --> 00:34:52.160
&gt;&gt; Thanks so much, Graham.

00:34:52.160 --> 00:34:55.430
It was great to hear from you today and
I've loved being in your class,

00:34:55.430 --> 00:34:59.350
and I take many of the things you've said
to heart and they've helped me.

00:34:59.350 --> 00:35:02.885
I'm curious when you're, like genie goal
or the thing

00:35:02.885 --> 00:35:06.268
you're going after, how do you balance
that with the

00:35:06.268 --> 00:35:09.048
people in your family and in your life who
are

00:35:09.048 --> 00:35:15.020
also going after their own things when you
have interdependent relationships?

00:35:15.020 --> 00:35:16.199
&gt;&gt; Yeah, it's a great question.

00:35:16.199 --> 00:35:20.750
I think just having a lot of communication
about what you're both going after

00:35:20.750 --> 00:35:24.889
and then what you're talking about though
I think is more of a tactic.

00:35:24.889 --> 00:35:28.910
I think you and Ben should [LAUGH] support
each other in trying

00:35:28.910 --> 00:35:33.910
to achieve what you're both trying to
achieve and encourage each other.

00:35:33.910 --> 00:35:37.224
And there might be times when you both
have to

00:35:37.224 --> 00:35:41.627
take on roles that you're going to have to
share the duties.

00:35:41.627 --> 00:35:43.709
But I don't think that that gets in the
way of

00:35:43.709 --> 00:35:47.216
you both clearly kind of walking toward
what you're want to be going after.

00:35:49.788 --> 00:35:51.656
&gt;&gt; I think Amanda, the next question.

00:35:51.656 --> 00:35:52.216
&gt;&gt; Yes.

00:35:57.265 --> 00:36:02.590
Hi, Graham, let's imagine for a minute
that the angel has been revealed.

00:36:02.590 --> 00:36:03.895
You've self-actualized.

00:36:03.895 --> 00:36:08.290
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; It's very nice lighting up here.

00:36:08.290 --> 00:36:10.867
I want to understand how you maintain
humility

00:36:10.867 --> 00:36:13.713
and approachability when you reach that
goal, if

00:36:13.713 --> 00:36:18.710
it is attainable, and to turn around and
be able to bring others up with you?

00:36:18.710 --> 00:36:21.800
&gt;&gt; I think one of the things that,

00:36:24.507 --> 00:36:26.693
I always thought was that, there's a

00:36:26.693 --> 00:36:29.700
great quote by Marianne Williamson, I'm
not going to

00:36:29.700 --> 00:36:31.953
get it right, but she basically says,

00:36:31.953 --> 00:36:36.350
you giving yourself permission gives other
people permission.

00:36:36.350 --> 00:36:39.780
You shining bright gives others permission
to shine bright.

00:36:39.780 --> 00:36:42.110
And I think there's so much truth in that.

00:36:42.110 --> 00:36:45.498
I mean, I think about the people that I
admire the most,

00:36:45.498 --> 00:36:50.340
Whitney Wolfe Herd came to our class this
year, and she's just herself.

00:36:50.340 --> 00:36:53.820
She just like, you can just feel her
energy in herself.

00:36:53.820 --> 00:36:56.134
And from my mind, it doesn't have anything

00:36:56.134 --> 00:36:58.511
to do with humility, it gave me permission
to

00:36:58.511 --> 00:37:00.948
say, wow, this is what someone who's
really

00:37:00.948 --> 00:37:03.339
fired up about what they want to do looks
like.

00:37:05.190 --> 00:37:07.578
In terms of, I think people that you

00:37:07.578 --> 00:37:11.650
perceive as arrogant are probably actually
not doing that.

00:37:11.650 --> 00:37:13.980
[LAUGH] They're probably trying to pretend
and be something else.

00:37:15.040 --> 00:37:18.718
The people that I know that I admire the
most, they actually are pretty humble

00:37:18.718 --> 00:37:20.586
and they're pretty happy with who they

00:37:20.586 --> 00:37:23.200
are, they're not trying to be something
different.

00:37:23.200 --> 00:37:29.596
So I think sometimes arrogance is almost
the opposite of being your full self.

00:37:32.653 --> 00:37:33.757
&gt;&gt; Jill?

00:37:38.282 --> 00:37:39.910
&gt;&gt; Hey, Graham.

00:37:39.910 --> 00:37:42.374
You've come such a long way from being
that

00:37:42.374 --> 00:37:45.625
skinny kid, striving to make it on the
wrestling team.

00:37:45.625 --> 00:37:46.062
&gt;&gt; Thank you.

00:37:46.062 --> 00:37:49.680
&gt;&gt; And [LAUGH]-
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH].

00:37:49.680 --> 00:37:52.810
&gt;&gt; My question is, during the-

00:37:52.810 --> 00:37:56.347
&gt;&gt; No, you can just keep going [LAUGH],
ask your question.

00:37:56.347 --> 00:37:59.975
&gt;&gt; My question is more, when you share
that story of friends coming

00:37:59.975 --> 00:38:04.585
to you, they're kind of all involved in
tech, they're going with the bubble-

00:38:04.585 --> 00:38:05.147
&gt;&gt; Right.

00:38:05.147 --> 00:38:09.412
&gt;&gt; And you're buying a label printing
company.

00:38:09.412 --> 00:38:10.370
&gt;&gt; Right.

00:38:10.370 --> 00:38:14.343
&gt;&gt; At that point, how do you have
conviction to keep doing what

00:38:14.343 --> 00:38:19.360
you're doing and not just follow the herd
and what everyone else is doing?

00:38:19.360 --> 00:38:21.120
&gt;&gt; Yeah, it's a great question.

00:38:21.120 --> 00:38:28.200
There is one person in my class who will
remain nameless, who follows every herd.

00:38:28.200 --> 00:38:32.330
It was dotcom, then it was hedge funds.

00:38:32.330 --> 00:38:35.588
And then, I think now he's doing a crypto
spark or something.

00:38:35.588 --> 00:38:36.850
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:38:36.850 --> 00:38:38.469
&gt;&gt; I'm actually not sure, I'm
exaggerating.

00:38:38.469 --> 00:38:39.799
I think I'm exaggerating.

00:38:39.799 --> 00:38:44.046
And [LAUGH] I think for me, at least,

00:38:44.046 --> 00:38:49.721
I could see some progress, it wasn't all
bad.

00:38:49.721 --> 00:38:54.440
There would be moments where I'd say, I
could kind of see how this might work out.

00:38:54.440 --> 00:38:57.067
And I was having some small little wins
along

00:38:57.067 --> 00:39:00.052
the way, which was enough to keep me
encouraged.

00:39:00.052 --> 00:39:02.890
And the other thing is, I got really

00:39:02.890 --> 00:39:06.863
fortunate that the very first three or
four people

00:39:06.863 --> 00:39:11.120
we hired at Alpine were just unbelievable
partners of

00:39:11.120 --> 00:39:14.452
mine, and they were in the swamp with me.

00:39:14.452 --> 00:39:17.770
[LAUGH] And there's a good expression, I
don't know who said

00:39:17.770 --> 00:39:21.840
it, which is like, a problem shared is a
problem halved.

00:39:21.840 --> 00:39:25.210
And we were just in it together.

00:39:25.210 --> 00:39:28.234
And there were days when I would have a
down day and I would need to get

00:39:28.234 --> 00:39:32.030
dragged out, and there are days when they
would, and I would need to drag them out.

00:39:32.030 --> 00:39:33.660
So that was a huge part.

00:39:33.660 --> 00:39:37.278
So I'd say those two things is just having
some great people with

00:39:37.278 --> 00:39:40.770
me and then just seeing a little bit of
progress along the way.

00:39:40.770 --> 00:39:44.580
And also having the conviction to not have
to follow and dive into the herd.

00:39:45.680 --> 00:39:48.860
I literally didn't understand a lot of it,
kind of,

00:39:48.860 --> 00:39:52.880
won't go off on my crypto tangent, but I
don't understand.

00:39:52.880 --> 00:39:57.043
I didn't understand a lot of that.

00:39:57.043 --> 00:40:00.159
It wasn't like I'm smarter than everyone,
I actually thought maybe I'm

00:40:00.159 --> 00:40:02.990
not as smart as everyone, but I stuck with
what I kind of understood.

00:40:05.400 --> 00:40:06.180
&gt;&gt; We're down here to Mackenzie.

00:40:11.203 --> 00:40:15.121
Thanks, Graham, I'm like right from the
center.

00:40:15.121 --> 00:40:15.900
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:40:15.900 --> 00:40:22.169
&gt;&gt; So, kind of getting on a little bit the
swamp point, I think looking back on

00:40:22.169 --> 00:40:28.357
your career, it's easier to say, just
stick it out, stay in the swamp.

00:40:28.357 --> 00:40:31.746
You're struggling, obviously it worked out
tremendously.

00:40:31.746 --> 00:40:34.123
How do you know?

00:40:34.123 --> 00:40:37.730
And we've heard a lot of stories from
people who've come to class of people who

00:40:37.730 --> 00:40:41.518
have tried one thing, it didn't work out,
they left that on the side of the road.

00:40:41.518 --> 00:40:43.088
They went and did something different or

00:40:43.088 --> 00:40:44.946
they pivoted their company to something
else.

00:40:44.946 --> 00:40:48.822
How do you know when the time is right to
just say, well, what advice do you give to

00:40:48.822 --> 00:40:50.931
people to say, hey, this is not working
and

00:40:50.931 --> 00:40:53.451
it's never going to and let me find
something else.

00:40:53.451 --> 00:40:55.436
&gt;&gt; Well, and that's a great question.

00:40:55.436 --> 00:40:58.850
There's a huge difference between pivoting
and

00:40:58.850 --> 00:41:02.604
deciding you're done, there's a big
difference.

00:41:02.604 --> 00:41:07.046
I mean we pivoted the whole time, we're
still pivoting.

00:41:07.046 --> 00:41:10.479
I mean, I think that's really healthy to
say, hey,

00:41:10.479 --> 00:41:14.004
this is working really great, let's do
more of that.

00:41:14.004 --> 00:41:15.559
And this isn't working, let's do less of
that.

00:41:15.559 --> 00:41:19.831
I mean, I think that that's just great
leadership.

00:41:19.831 --> 00:41:22.621
I mean, that's what you're supposed to do.

00:41:22.621 --> 00:41:24.844
What's different is, so often what I

00:41:24.844 --> 00:41:27.838
see is like, imagine you're running a
marathon.

00:41:27.838 --> 00:41:31.744
I see people run 25 miles and they don't
know

00:41:31.744 --> 00:41:36.165
how close they are and they turn around
and run back.

00:41:36.165 --> 00:41:40.008
[LAUGH] Which is 25 miles back when they
only had

00:41:40.008 --> 00:41:45.017
to go one more season math, they just
finished the marathon.

00:41:45.017 --> 00:41:47.186
So I think it's not about sticking with

00:41:47.186 --> 00:41:50.014
something that's not working, I think it's
more

00:41:50.014 --> 00:41:52.711
just having the confidence that you're
going to

00:41:52.711 --> 00:41:54.963
figure out the answer with a few pivots.

00:41:54.963 --> 00:41:57.350
One thing I'll just as an aside the thing,

00:41:57.350 --> 00:42:00.002
the ones kind of question that we've asked
that

00:42:00.002 --> 00:42:02.655
has made us more successful maybe than any
other

00:42:02.655 --> 00:42:05.660
question is, what's working that we can do
more of?

00:42:05.660 --> 00:42:08.855
Most people focus on what's wrong and
what's not going right.

00:42:08.855 --> 00:42:10.493
But it kind of goes back to the angel

00:42:10.493 --> 00:42:13.094
in the marble, which is like, there's
actually some

00:42:13.094 --> 00:42:15.524
things that we're really great at that
we're true

00:42:15.524 --> 00:42:17.579
to us that are things that make us
special.

00:42:17.579 --> 00:42:19.112
Let's go all in on that.

00:42:19.112 --> 00:42:23.751
So, for example, at Alpine, I would say
I'm an average investor.

00:42:23.751 --> 00:42:26.335
I don't really have a ton of energy for
deals,

00:42:26.335 --> 00:42:29.117
like that's not really what gets me fired
up, but

00:42:29.117 --> 00:42:32.031
I'd say, the thing that gets me excited is
people,

00:42:32.031 --> 00:42:34.904
and talent, and we build a whole firm
around that.

00:42:34.904 --> 00:42:37.069
We leaned into what we're good at, so

00:42:37.069 --> 00:42:39.785
I think giving yourself permission to do
that.

00:42:42.529 --> 00:42:43.463
&gt;&gt; Kate.

00:42:43.463 --> 00:42:44.653
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:42:47.290 --> 00:42:49.400
Hi Graham, thank you so much for this
beautiful lecture.

00:42:49.400 --> 00:42:55.827
My question is about reconciliation of
this call to action and

00:42:55.827 --> 00:43:02.509
follow your path and your passion and your
cause and sacrifice.

00:43:02.509 --> 00:43:07.652
When sometimes things happen and things
change drastically, it's

00:43:07.652 --> 00:43:11.386
another call to respond to some inner
values too.

00:43:11.386 --> 00:43:18.029
Maybe some traumatic experiences in your
family or something else.

00:43:18.029 --> 00:43:24.339
How do you reconcile this, maybe time that
you take away from

00:43:24.339 --> 00:43:30.409
your path, or energy, or anything else on
your journey?

00:43:30.409 --> 00:43:32.424
&gt;&gt; Yeah, well, first off, I'm sorry

00:43:32.424 --> 00:43:35.167
if that's what you're going through right
now.

00:43:35.167 --> 00:43:38.012
And so I'm really sorry to hear that.

00:43:38.012 --> 00:43:41.993
But what I'd say is what you're describing
actually is your call.

00:43:41.993 --> 00:43:43.604
That's your louder call.

00:43:43.604 --> 00:43:48.475
And you're actually doing, you're giving
that, you're listening to that

00:43:48.475 --> 00:43:52.700
call, and that's the call that you should
be answering right now.

00:43:52.700 --> 00:43:55.030
My call is, I'm not saying to anybody that

00:43:55.030 --> 00:43:58.084
you should be an entrepreneur, you should
be a leader.

00:43:58.084 --> 00:44:00.802
All I'm saying is you should listen to
whatever is true for

00:44:00.802 --> 00:44:03.692
you, whatever in your heart you know what
the right thing is.

00:44:03.692 --> 00:44:06.131
And it sounds for you right now, that
might be your

00:44:06.131 --> 00:44:09.164
family, so I think that's exactly what you
should be doing.

00:44:09.164 --> 00:44:14.479
And then when it's time, you'll decide if
you want to listen to another

00:44:14.479 --> 00:44:19.543
call at that time, but it sounds like you
know the answer right now.

00:44:23.357 --> 00:44:24.456
&gt;&gt; Karen.

00:44:31.235 --> 00:44:31.921
&gt;&gt; Thank you, Graham.

00:44:31.921 --> 00:44:38.070
You mentioned 99% of the people don't
figure out what they really want to do.

00:44:38.070 --> 00:44:40.827
You and others at this school and in our
lives have been

00:44:40.827 --> 00:44:43.962
supporting us on this journey to figure
out what we want to do.

00:44:43.962 --> 00:44:47.464
Going forward, how can we continue to help
others in our

00:44:47.464 --> 00:44:50.903
life that are maybe not in this room to
figure that out?

00:44:50.903 --> 00:44:55.233
&gt;&gt; Yeah, well, the biggest thing you can
do and this is for all of

00:44:55.233 --> 00:45:00.335
you or people you want to help is just
make make space to ask the questions.

00:45:00.335 --> 00:45:03.917
Because, you have this crazy, all of you
when you get.

00:45:03.917 --> 00:45:05.872
A couple months from now, I know this

00:45:05.872 --> 00:45:08.342
is scary, you're going to actually be at
work.

00:45:08.342 --> 00:45:12.329
And you might have back to back Zoom calls
for ten hours, it's really

00:45:12.329 --> 00:45:15.862
easy just to get swept up in that, and you
need to make space.

00:45:15.862 --> 00:45:19.198
You need to put two hours on the calendar,
and maybe you have a

00:45:19.198 --> 00:45:23.467
coach, or maybe you have a couple friends,
you need to ask those questions.

00:45:23.467 --> 00:45:25.654
And it doesn't always come out of the
first session or

00:45:25.654 --> 00:45:27.851
the second one or the third one, it might
take weeks.

00:45:27.851 --> 00:45:31.851
But you have to make space to listen
effectively.

00:45:31.851 --> 00:45:34.095
And so the thing you could give to people

00:45:34.095 --> 00:45:37.293
you're trying to help is space to ask
these questions.

00:45:37.293 --> 00:45:43.115
&gt;&gt; I think our next question was back
here.

00:45:43.115 --> 00:45:45.373
&gt;&gt; Good afternoon, Graham, thank you.

00:45:45.373 --> 00:45:48.261
From someone who never made enough space
yet,

00:45:48.261 --> 00:45:51.297
I'm very intrigued in your firm and
yourself and

00:45:51.297 --> 00:45:53.815
how you encourage people and you focus on

00:45:53.815 --> 00:45:56.722
people and help them uncover what they can
be.

00:45:56.722 --> 00:45:58.827
What coaching do you give in terms of

00:45:58.827 --> 00:46:02.896
balance between pursuing what you can be
identifying, consuming,

00:46:02.896 --> 00:46:05.349
and committing to what you can be, and the

00:46:05.349 --> 00:46:09.088
balance of whatever everything else in
life demands, right?

00:46:09.088 --> 00:46:12.202
Do you see your team members struggling
with

00:46:12.202 --> 00:46:15.324
that balance, and what advice do you give?

00:46:15.324 --> 00:46:18.876
&gt;&gt; Yeah, and just to be clear, it's like
the bounce of just how

00:46:18.876 --> 00:46:21.735
much they're throwing themselves into work
and

00:46:21.735 --> 00:46:24.257
that they have other stuff that goes away.

00:46:24.257 --> 00:46:30.676
One way to think about this is this is a
framework is that the

00:46:30.676 --> 00:46:36.964
amount of work that you have is infinite
and you could fill

00:46:36.964 --> 00:46:43.260
your entire life with it and you're never
going to be done.

00:46:43.260 --> 00:46:46.989
That's also kind of depressing but the
reason I'm bringing it up is if you

00:46:46.989 --> 00:46:50.732
say, I'm going to go to my son's football
game when I'm done with my work.

00:46:50.732 --> 00:46:54.560
Or I'm going to make time to exercise as
soon as I'm done with work.

00:46:54.560 --> 00:46:58.794
Or I'm going to go to my friend's wedding
when I'm done with work.

00:46:58.794 --> 00:47:03.749
Like, just realize you're never done with
work, there is no

00:47:03.749 --> 00:47:09.010
such thing for someone like you who's
driven and wants to go far.

00:47:09.010 --> 00:47:12.258
And so you have to put those other things
in first, you have

00:47:12.258 --> 00:47:16.324
to say, I'm going to go to the son's game,
or the wedding, or whatever.

00:47:16.324 --> 00:47:19.382
And then the work is going to have to kind
of work around that.

00:47:19.382 --> 00:47:23.630
because if you don't put those things in,
work will just consume everything.

00:47:23.630 --> 00:47:30.732
Because you're never done, there's so much
that you can do at all times.

00:47:30.732 --> 00:47:33.970
So for some reason that framework has
helped me a lot, not

00:47:33.970 --> 00:47:38.104
ever feel like, I'm going to at some point
be done because you're not.

00:47:42.408 --> 00:47:46.730
&gt;&gt; Melissa Parkinson.

00:47:46.730 --> 00:47:48.056
&gt;&gt; Hey, Graham, I'm Melissa.

00:47:48.056 --> 00:47:52.657
I was lucky to take your managerial skills
class way back in the fall last year.

00:47:52.657 --> 00:47:56.910
And I'd love if you could re-share the top
mistakes you saw your

00:47:56.910 --> 00:48:02.369
classmates make 20 years out because that
was something that really stayed with me.

00:48:02.369 --> 00:48:05.271
&gt;&gt; Top mistakes my classmates made.

00:48:05.271 --> 00:48:07.160
I mean I think I shared a.

00:48:07.160 --> 00:48:12.520
Most of them in this talk, which kind of
gets swept up.

00:48:12.520 --> 00:48:20.312
The two biggest, most dangerous words that
you or my classmates ever say are not now.

00:48:20.312 --> 00:48:24.552
If you hear yourself saying those words,
just be really, really careful.

00:48:24.552 --> 00:48:28.445
Be afraid because not now is really fear

00:48:28.445 --> 00:48:32.933
kind of in another word or in another
format.

00:48:32.933 --> 00:48:41.043
And it's very dangerous because, as I said
earlier, you become your environment.

00:48:41.043 --> 00:48:44.071
The people you're with, their values
become yours.

00:48:44.071 --> 00:48:47.851
And so mistake number one that I saw my
classmates make is

00:48:47.851 --> 00:48:51.723
just putting it off and putting it off and
putting it off.

00:48:56.130 --> 00:48:59.898
And then the other things would be things
I talked about in

00:48:59.898 --> 00:49:04.312
the talk, which would be listening to your
head, not your heart.

00:49:04.312 --> 00:49:07.790
And never really deciding what it is
you're excited about or what you want.

00:49:07.790 --> 00:49:09.834
Those would be the probably the biggest
ones I've seen.

00:49:12.010 --> 00:49:15.639
&gt;&gt; Great, we have time for one or two more
questions if there are any.

00:49:15.639 --> 00:49:16.512
We have Kevin.

00:49:20.454 --> 00:49:22.404
&gt;&gt; Hi Graham, Kevin here.

00:49:22.404 --> 00:49:25.061
So you're a big fan of self development.

00:49:25.061 --> 00:49:28.063
So I wonder what do you think about
improving on

00:49:28.063 --> 00:49:32.538
your weakness or development area, and
working on your strengths.

00:49:32.538 --> 00:49:34.793
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I'm a huge fan of working

00:49:34.793 --> 00:49:39.069
on your strengths, because that's what's
uniquely you.

00:49:39.069 --> 00:49:43.089
That's where your superpower is, is your
strength.

00:49:43.089 --> 00:49:46.407
And what's interesting is, I always
remember thinking, well this is

00:49:46.407 --> 00:49:49.911
my strength, I'm sure it's everyone else's
strength, and it's not.

00:49:49.911 --> 00:49:54.195
The thing that you love, Naval Ravikant
always says, what's the thing

00:49:54.195 --> 00:49:57.721
that for you is play that for everyone
else feels like work?

00:49:57.721 --> 00:50:00.951
That's a good indication of where you
should be spending your energy.

00:50:00.951 --> 00:50:02.993
because you'll always be better at that
than

00:50:02.993 --> 00:50:05.287
anyone else, because you'll just do it for
fun.

00:50:05.287 --> 00:50:08.760
For me that's teaching, I mean, my wife
can tell you that.

00:50:08.760 --> 00:50:13.060
I'm walking around in my house, working on
lectures, and giving this

00:50:13.060 --> 00:50:16.299
talk that I just gave to my pillows in the
living room.

00:50:16.299 --> 00:50:20.503
And because that's play for me, that's
fun.

00:50:20.503 --> 00:50:23.214
And so I think leaning into your things

00:50:23.214 --> 00:50:26.702
that really give you energy is your
superpower.

00:50:26.702 --> 00:50:32.020
There will be weaknesses that could
completely take you out of the game.

00:50:32.020 --> 00:50:35.095
Usually that has to do with self awareness
and how

00:50:35.095 --> 00:50:38.555
you're showing up and some kind of hard
edges you have.

00:50:38.555 --> 00:50:41.768
That's usually the weakness that takes
people out of the game.

00:50:41.768 --> 00:50:44.580
It's not usually intellect or horsepower,
its

00:50:44.580 --> 00:50:47.126
ability to get along with people, really.

00:50:47.126 --> 00:50:50.646
And so that's one that you probably need
to work on that enough

00:50:50.646 --> 00:50:53.085
that you're not going to like shoot
yourself

00:50:53.085 --> 00:50:55.334
in the foot playing to your strengths.

00:50:55.334 --> 00:51:01.643
And that's the one that trips probably the
most people up that I've seen.

00:51:01.643 --> 00:51:02.939
&gt;&gt; And our last question back here.

00:51:07.324 --> 00:51:13.173
&gt;&gt; Hi Graham, I was incredibly grateful to
be one of your TAs earlier this year.

00:51:13.173 --> 00:51:17.796
And I saw firsthand your commitment to DE
and I, diversity, equity and inclusion.

00:51:17.796 --> 00:51:19.643
And all of the things that you do to

00:51:19.643 --> 00:51:22.679
bring other voices into the room or other
opinions into

00:51:22.679 --> 00:51:25.254
the room for people that don't look like
you,

00:51:25.254 --> 00:51:28.159
sound like you, or have the same
experience as you.

00:51:28.159 --> 00:51:31.540
And I'm thinking more about Karen's
question of how can we support other

00:51:31.540 --> 00:51:35.152
people and whatnot who are not necessarily
involved in the same circles as us?

00:51:35.152 --> 00:51:38.431
And I was just wondering could you say a
word about what you do to

00:51:38.431 --> 00:51:42.676
learn or continue to hear from people who
don't look like you, sound like you?

00:51:42.676 --> 00:51:45.778
And then what we could do to incorporate
that into how we

00:51:45.778 --> 00:51:50.376
are showing up to boardrooms, meeting
rooms, pitches, whatever it might be.

00:51:50.376 --> 00:51:52.863
So it's really a question about learning
from diverse voices.

00:51:52.863 --> 00:51:54.661
&gt;&gt; Yeah, that's an awesome question.

00:51:54.661 --> 00:51:59.313
By the way, thanks for the kind words,
Connor.

00:51:59.313 --> 00:52:06.935
I mean, I'll tell you just an example that
we've learned over the years.

00:52:06.935 --> 00:52:08.895
And I'll use it as an example of
companies.

00:52:08.895 --> 00:52:11.569
Rather than talk in generalities, I'll use
a real example.

00:52:11.569 --> 00:52:16.518
So when we go into a business, last year

00:52:16.518 --> 00:52:21.620
we bought 127 businesses last year.

00:52:21.620 --> 00:52:24.941
And when we go in, most often we are
actually putting in a new

00:52:24.941 --> 00:52:27.932
leadership team, because that's what the
business

00:52:27.932 --> 00:52:30.339
needs and we're very upfront about that.

00:52:30.339 --> 00:52:33.990
The first move we make is to actually

00:52:33.990 --> 00:52:39.609
enroll everyone in the company in where
we're going.

00:52:39.609 --> 00:52:42.359
We have this expression that the answer's
always in the room.

00:52:42.359 --> 00:52:44.296
We don't have the answer, there's no top
down.

00:52:44.296 --> 00:52:48.680
It is literally enrolling everybody in
this in this conversation.

00:52:48.680 --> 00:52:54.733
And I remember when I took over a
business, around 2010 I became CEO.

00:52:54.733 --> 00:52:57.810
And I did this, I went on a listening tour

00:52:57.810 --> 00:53:00.991
and I sat there with a yellow pad and a
pen.

00:53:00.991 --> 00:53:05.487
And I went and met with most of the
leaders in the company,

00:53:05.487 --> 00:53:09.718
and a lot of the people that were on the
ground as well.

00:53:09.718 --> 00:53:12.249
And I sat down and the most common thing
that they

00:53:12.249 --> 00:53:15.367
ever said was, no one's ever asked me my
opinion before.

00:53:15.367 --> 00:53:19.270
[LAUGH] And the ideas were there, I didn't
have to

00:53:19.270 --> 00:53:23.664
be brilliant, I just had to give those
voices oxygen.

00:53:23.664 --> 00:53:25.910
And not only did I get a better

00:53:25.910 --> 00:53:30.889
answer from that, but everybody felt like
they contributed.

00:53:30.889 --> 00:53:34.015
And that they were enrolled in the
solution because they

00:53:34.015 --> 00:53:37.552
had been asked and they'd been invited to
that conversation.

00:53:37.552 --> 00:53:41.955
So you really just have to listen and you
don't have to be brilliant.

00:53:41.955 --> 00:53:44.760
The people that you're going to lead, the
people that

00:53:44.760 --> 00:53:48.024
you're going to be involved with, they
will have the answer.

00:53:48.024 --> 00:53:53.013
And you just have to kind of make sure
you're giving their voices some oxygen.

